00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:01.300
of Motor Vehicles.

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.510
But in all seriousness, machine
learning and machine vision

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.130
really do have the
potential to change

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:08.210
the world for the better.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:11.680
So what does peanuts and
almonds have anything

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:14.290
to do with machine learning?

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:17.650
The answer is machine vision.

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:22.540
Machine vision today is a
$10 billion global industry,

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.290
and is expected to be
$18 billion by 2025.

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:27.910
In North America
alone, the industry

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:31.810
has grown 10% this past year.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:36.770
This market is really broken
up into two product categories.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:41.050
We have the PC based systems
and all in one smart cameras.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:44.760
These PC based systems have a
lot of horsepower capability.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.400
You have the capability of
plugging in multiple cameras,

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.997
being able to run all
the cameras in parallel,

00:03:52.997 --> 00:03:54.580
and then you also
have the flexibility

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.490
of being able to swap
out lenses and lighting

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:01.870
to cater it to your vision
inspection application.

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:06.050
And on the other side, we have
the all in one smart cameras.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.290
They're a really
nice, tight package

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:10.330
that have everything built in.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:15.340
They have the processing, the
lighting, and the lensing,

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:17.200
all in a nice form factor.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.570
But you do lose out
on the horsepower,

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:22.420
and you are limited to,
obviously, the one unit

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:23.170
for your location.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.620
Now, these are
real life examples

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.220
that I have up on screen.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:35.160
The first example
is a medical device

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.610
that we were doing 100%
inspection on these products

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.480
as they were going out the door.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.990
And it's a silicone
injection molded part that

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.850
was prone to under molding.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:48.900
And this under
molding would be--

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:52.740
not enough material was injected
in the injection mold process.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.050
And so we had two cameras
mounted perpendicular

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:58.950
to each other, taking a picture
simultaneously, and getting

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:03.370
a throughput of one per
second on this operation.

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:06.760
The second example is an
automotive application.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:10.170
This is an electronic device
that goes into vehicles.

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:13.920
And there's a process that
requires thermal paste being

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:17.370
applied to this product before
it goes into the automated

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:18.450
machinery.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.820
And so we decided
to use an all in one

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.980
smart camera for
this application

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:25.860
to detect that thermal paste
and give the robot the OK

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.720
to go ahead with the operation.

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:32.820
Another way these cameras are
used is for robot guidance.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:37.680
This is an example of a camera
mounted on the end of a robot

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.930
to give it coordinates
on where it should go.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:42.828
And this is doing
server assembly,

00:05:42.828 --> 00:05:44.370
and we were looking
at some fiducials

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.640
on the printed circuit board to
decide where those parts could

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.660
go with great accuracy.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:53.570
And other examples might
be picking and placing

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:57.770
car bumpers from a rack
onto a moving assembly line,

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:01.490
picking and placing horns onto
a sub assembly, inspecting

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:04.820
a bead of adhesive
to verify that it's

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:08.390
a solid bead of
adhesive, and then

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:10.685
being able to read
barcodes and OCR.

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:19.530
Here I have up on screen two
examples of today's programming

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:20.880
interfaces.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.810
And on the left I have a-- this
is more of a drag and drop.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:29.370
This is, you have your palette
of tools that you would drag

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:31.740
and drop onto your screen
and create your program

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:33.760
in a tree-like structure.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.330
And over on the right
hand side is an example

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:38.460
of more of a spreadsheet
style programming, where

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:40.290
you can import
functions and do more

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:43.380
of a programming
in a spreadsheet.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:48.750
Both are based on the idea of
having a palette of tool sets,

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:52.870
being able to pull those
tool sets into your program,

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:54.990
then manipulate them.

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:57.930
And the thought has
always been to--

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:01.140
in order to add more
capability, add more tool sets.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:06.970
Now, I'm going to quickly go
through kind of today's process

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:10.630
on how we program
vision systems today.

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:12.130
And essentially
what we're doing is,

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:16.390
we're looking at a bitmap
image of a picture.

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:18.760
There have been
advancements in the industry

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:21.520
to triangulate between
bitmap locations

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:27.280
to give you higher accuracy that
this is the image that you're

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:27.958
looking for.

00:07:27.958 --> 00:07:29.500
But essentially what
we're looking at

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:33.280
is taking an image bitmap
and comparing it to one

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:36.100
that we've trained against.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:39.430
First step is to
define your camera

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:44.290
lighting and lensing, and
adjusting your aperture

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:46.030
for your application.

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:47.530
Then we can go into
the programming.

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:50.530
And programming, one of the
first steps we typically do

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:52.060
is the calibration.

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:54.880
If it's going to be used
in coordinated motion

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:57.010
between a camera
and a robot, you

00:07:57.010 --> 00:07:59.020
want to be able to
take those coordinates

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:01.750
and give them real
life coordinates

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:04.460
that the robot can interpret.

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:06.920
Then you'll use, just like
we talked about a minute ago,

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:09.020
we'll use the drag and
drop from our palette

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:12.360
on the screen to
create our program.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.170
For example, these palette tools
might include a circle tool.

00:08:15.170 --> 00:08:18.440
If you were trying to define a
golf ball moving down assembly

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:21.750
line, you might use a circle
tool to tell the system, hey,

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:23.690
I'm looking for round object.

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:29.420
Or a dispense bead, you might
use the line tool for something

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:30.890
like that.

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:33.230
Or a histogram tool if you're
trying to figure out how

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:35.780
bright or dark something is.

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:38.090
And you'll add these tools
and create your program.

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:40.850
And you might include
some pre filters

00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:44.930
to make those pixels pop
out a little bit better.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:49.350
And then it's ready to run.

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:51.620
And we run through the
snap and find routine.

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:56.630
And it's not really until we
get to those thousandth one

00:08:56.630 --> 00:08:59.810
that we really feel comfortable
with hey, this program

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:04.430
that we created is going to
work for our assembly line.

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:06.770
And keep in mind
that false positives

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:10.630
are a part of life
in this industry.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:11.800
So why is that?

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:17.810
Well, the systems today are
suspect to lighting conditions.

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:22.430
We have-- imagine you're
in your factory floor,

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:24.140
you have a skylight,
and the lighting

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:25.670
changes throughout the day.

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:28.040
Well, you have to account
for that in your software

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:31.490
program and your vision program
so that, as those lighting

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:34.460
conditions change, that you
are still successfully finding

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:37.400
the model as you intended.

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.410
Camera orientation is important.

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:42.230
If that camera
happened to get bumped,

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:44.390
that is an environmental
issue that you

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:46.020
might have to overcome.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:50.330
And, like I had mentioned
earlier that the operators--

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:52.730
because you have
these false positives,

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:56.510
operators on the assembly line
tend to open up tolerances

00:09:56.510 --> 00:09:59.930
that you've built into
your software program

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:03.390
to alleviate their jobs to
get the product out the door.

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:05.240
But what they're really
doing is opening up

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:07.760
the opportunity
for failed product

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:10.820
to be going out the door.

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:15.140
It's also very difficult
to detect any object

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:16.190
with variability.

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:19.430
Like you saw earlier with
the peanuts, anything

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:22.370
organic like fruit
is very difficult.

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:25.670
Castings are another
difficult object

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:29.120
to find, because sometimes
they come out shiny, or dull,

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:32.270
or have additional
flashing on them.

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:37.100
And logging of data
is such a value,

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:39.530
and it's underserved
in the market

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:43.830
today, mainly because
it's not accessible.

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:47.840
The application
is there, but it's

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:50.510
up to the engineers that
are building their software

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:54.290
programs to make that
connection to the cloud.

00:10:58.370 --> 00:11:02.400
What if vision systems were
less difficult to maintain,

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:05.260
easier to share programs
across factory floors,

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:08.820
camera resolutions weren't
as critical, lighting

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:12.200
and environmental variables
were less of an issue,

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:15.260
systems were less expensive,
and the same system

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:19.380
that you're at home sorting
LEGOs with your children

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:22.940
is the same system that you're
using at an automotive factory

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:24.500
to detect scratches on trim?

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:29.260
Is open source the solution?

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:34.210
Well, TensorFlow is a great
object detection tool.

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:37.660
OpenCV is good for
measurement and calibration.

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:40.840
Node-RED is a good
logic control and allows

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:44.200
you to connect to
peripheral devices.

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:47.350
Tesseract OCR is the number
one optical recognition

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:51.430
software available today,
and ZBar is a good tool

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:53.380
for reading barcodes.

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:57.070
But not one of these
is a silver bullet.

00:11:57.070 --> 00:12:01.940
Each one of them comes with
its own steep learning curve,

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:05.062
and integration between
them is non-existent.

00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:14.230
We wanted to crack this
nut by democratizing

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:16.900
the industrial vision market.

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:19.480
Removing the technical
barriers to entry,

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:22.330
literally harnessing
the ML Engine

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:26.950
and bringing it on Prem when
needed, simplifying the setup,

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:31.412
creating an all in one, easy to
use graphical user interface.

00:12:31.412 --> 00:12:34.130
I wanted to make it
flexible and modular

00:12:34.130 --> 00:12:41.150
by providing an open API, being
able to use any USB camera,

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:45.350
and keeping it low cost
by keeping it open source,

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:50.360
using low cost CPUs
in edge devices,

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:54.540
being able to train in the
cloud instead of on Prem.

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:00.200
Integrated cloud and
open source is the future

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:01.240
to industrial vision.

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:07.060
We are super excited to be
releasing an open source

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:11.170
version of Flexible Vision which
meets all of our democratizing

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:11.950
objectives.

00:13:11.950 --> 00:13:14.620
It has endless market
potential, opens

00:13:14.620 --> 00:13:19.160
the door to use cases that
weren't previously available,

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:21.460
whether it be to
pricing constraints

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:25.000
or functionality in the market.

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:31.730
Vision models can now be shared
across factory floors globally,

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:34.120
and version control is built in.

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:37.780
The system is automation ready
and can communicate directly

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:41.350
with robots and PLCs
on factory floors.

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:43.160
I have a short
five minute video.

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:45.940
I'm going to go
through the software,

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:47.870
and then I'm going to
pass it over to Sam.

00:13:47.870 --> 00:13:49.757
And Sam's really going
to go into the ins

00:13:49.757 --> 00:13:51.340
and outs of the
software and how we're

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:54.993
using Google Cloud and
Google components to bring it

00:13:54.993 --> 00:13:55.535
all together.

00:13:59.163 --> 00:14:01.080
So the very first thing
that we're going to do

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:03.540
is we're going to
plug-in a USB camera.

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:08.070
And it shows up automatically
under connected devices.

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:10.740
And then you'll see
we can see the camera,

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:13.000
and we're going to
change the resolution.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:16.260
We can go up to 4k
resolution on this.

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:18.870
But keep in mind that
the lower the resolution,

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:21.850
the faster the processing.

00:14:21.850 --> 00:14:24.540
And this is an optional step,
but the camera calibration

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:27.420
is required for robot guidance.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:32.010
So we can take
the pixel location

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:34.260
and convert it to
millimeters, and also

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:36.990
take any SKU and
angle rotation, so we

00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:39.070
could pass that
information to the robot

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:41.137
so it can pick it up correctly.

00:14:41.137 --> 00:14:42.970
And then if we go back
to the camera screen,

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:45.060
we can see that
it is calibrated,

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:47.670
and we could see the conversion
from pixels to millimeters,

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:50.460
and the rotation
that we saw earlier.

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:51.920
All right, now the fun stuff.

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:53.670
We're going to create
a project, and we're

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:55.003
going to call this Nut Training.

00:15:00.520 --> 00:15:04.000
And the next step
is to start taking

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:08.090
some images of these nuts.

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:10.630
So we're going to
select the camera

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:12.790
and select the project
that we want to associate

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:13.810
all these images to.

00:15:16.950 --> 00:15:19.675
And we're going to sprinkle
out some peanuts, randomly

00:15:19.675 --> 00:15:20.175
oriented.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:26.522
Take a snapshot of that.

00:15:26.522 --> 00:15:28.230
And then we'll sprinkle
out some almonds.

00:15:37.552 --> 00:15:39.635
And we'll do that one more
time with some walnuts.

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:50.800
And now we're going to move
into the image annotation.

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:53.400
This is where we're
going to tag the images

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:55.920
and tell the system what
each one of these items are.

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.080
So we do have some
icons over on the left

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:04.450
that tell us that these images
have not been tagged yet.

00:16:04.450 --> 00:16:08.895
And we have a whole lot of nice
tool sets built into this GUI.

00:16:08.895 --> 00:16:10.270
But the very first
thing we'll do

00:16:10.270 --> 00:16:11.520
is we'll create the tag names.

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:14.350
And this tag is going to,
first of all, be peanuts.

00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:17.675
And we're going to
do a box detection.

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:26.310
And just simply drag and drop.

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:28.435
We're going to go through
and select these peanuts.

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:39.825
And then we'll do the
same thing for almonds.

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:47.440
And like you saw earlier when
we were creating these tags,

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:51.130
there were a few other checkbox
we could have put in there.

00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:54.400
There's object detection,
which we're doing right now.

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:59.720
We have OCR and barcode
reading all built in.

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:02.740
We also have some other tool
sets like a zoom feature, which

00:17:02.740 --> 00:17:04.599
allows you to, if
you're trying to do

00:17:04.599 --> 00:17:08.680
a small components on a PCB
that were difficult to see,

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:11.680
we have this lure that
you can drag and drop.

00:17:15.470 --> 00:17:19.800
And now we're ready to start
the augmentation process.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:22.190
This is where we're going to
take just these three images

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:24.770
and augment them and
give them some variables

00:17:24.770 --> 00:17:30.755
to create thousands of images
from these just three images.

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:42.480
And click Export Data.

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:45.552
Here we have a pop up box,
which gives us some variables

00:17:45.552 --> 00:17:47.010
that we could punch
in if we wanted

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:49.470
to change how these images
were being augmented,

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:53.280
if we want to be able to rotate
them or scale them differently,

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:54.780
we can put those
parameters in here.

00:17:54.780 --> 00:17:56.930
But the default settings
seem to work pretty well.

00:17:59.598 --> 00:18:01.390
And you'll notice at
the top of the screen,

00:18:01.390 --> 00:18:05.070
we have an indicator that says,
hey, something's going on.

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:07.650
This pre training is a
pretty quick process.

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:10.018
It only takes 30 seconds or so.

00:18:10.018 --> 00:18:11.810
And you can see that
that's been completed.

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:23.500
And we can go to
the image data set,

00:18:23.500 --> 00:18:26.015
and you can see that now we
have those thousands of images

00:18:26.015 --> 00:18:26.515
created.

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:37.970
Then we're going to take
these augmented images

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:41.780
and export them
for model training.

00:18:41.780 --> 00:18:43.970
Again, we have a
small dialog box that

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:45.470
allows you to change
a few settings,

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:56.140
but we'll just keep
it, again, as default.

00:18:56.140 --> 00:18:58.660
And this process
happens up in the cloud,

00:18:58.660 --> 00:19:00.760
and it can take anywhere
between 20 minutes

00:19:00.760 --> 00:19:03.370
to an hour and a half, depending
on the model that you're

00:19:03.370 --> 00:19:04.270
training.

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:06.370
And we will come back,
and now we have--

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:10.790
we'll see that if we go to our--

00:19:10.790 --> 00:19:16.580
excuse me-- list projects, we'll
see that the project is done,

00:19:16.580 --> 00:19:18.722
and we can assign a model to it.

00:19:18.722 --> 00:19:20.180
The reason we did
it this way is we

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:22.580
wanted to have that
version control built in.

00:19:22.580 --> 00:19:24.860
So in a future date, as
you're refining your models

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:26.340
and making them
better and better,

00:19:26.340 --> 00:19:28.700
that you can go back
and update that.

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:33.110
And now we're ready.

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:35.060
That's really the last
step, and now we're

00:19:35.060 --> 00:19:37.970
ready just to capture and run.

00:19:37.970 --> 00:19:39.973
Select the camera.

00:19:39.973 --> 00:19:42.140
Select the project that
we're interested in running.

00:19:47.983 --> 00:19:48.900
And there you have it.

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:52.100
It's that easy to
create your models.

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:55.628
This is-- you'll see
in just a minute here,

00:19:55.628 --> 00:19:57.670
we're going to break apart
some of these walnuts,

00:19:57.670 --> 00:20:00.860
and the detection is
absolutely amazing.

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:03.310
And with that, I'm going
to pass it over to Sam.

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:04.935
SAM SILVERBERG: So
what I'm going to do

00:20:04.935 --> 00:20:07.840
is, I'm going to go through
some of the concepts that

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:10.660
were required to be
understood in order to make

00:20:10.660 --> 00:20:13.870
all this happen as
well as the underlying

00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:17.110
architecture from both and on
Prem and from a Google Cloud

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:19.300
perspective.

00:20:19.300 --> 00:20:22.150
But before I do, let's talk
a little bit about Flexible

00:20:22.150 --> 00:20:26.780
Vision and what Flexible
Assembly is releasing today.

00:20:26.780 --> 00:20:28.390
And it's an open
source package that

00:20:28.390 --> 00:20:33.670
provides end to end training
all the way through prediction.

00:20:33.670 --> 00:20:36.430
That includes
camera calibration.

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:40.660
It also includes object
detection, object localization,

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:44.350
OCR scanning, and
barcode scanning.

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:48.580
And it leverages ML Engine in
the cloud, Google Kubernetes

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:51.610
Engine to perform that
augmentation and training

00:20:51.610 --> 00:20:56.110
process, Google Cloud Storage
to store the images, obviously,

00:20:56.110 --> 00:20:59.860
in persistence, and
then finally, on Prem,

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:01.458
we're doing the prediction.

00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:06.900
This is what the overall
architecture looks like,

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:09.420
and specifically, what
you saw in the case

00:21:09.420 --> 00:21:12.480
of the initial
video Aaron showed,

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:14.840
which is that, all the
way on the left here--

00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:18.330
so on the left,
yeah, on the left

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:23.250
is the robot or the IoT devices
that you will be manipulating

00:21:23.250 --> 00:21:25.020
the data from the camera.

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:28.080
In the middle, or
the next over, what

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:29.970
you have is the workflow engine.

00:21:29.970 --> 00:21:34.440
The workflow engine, in the case
of what we did, is Node-RED.

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:36.600
so Node-RED is a
nice drag and drop

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:39.120
tool to do IoT
integration, but it

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:41.940
can be any host of
different tools there.

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:44.670
And then you have your
local prediction server.

00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:46.920
In case of Flexible
Vision, microservices

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:48.450
are heavily leveraged.

00:21:48.450 --> 00:21:50.520
And each one of the
different components

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:53.890
is actually a docker
container in and of itself.

00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:57.990
Now, prediction locally on
Prem is important, specifically

00:21:57.990 --> 00:22:02.880
for industrial applications,
because you need very quick,

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:05.340
sub 100 milliseconds
cycle times.

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:07.260
That round trip
time to the cloud

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:11.360
may not suffice in
those circumstances.

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:13.070
And finally, all the
way to the right,

00:22:13.070 --> 00:22:15.770
you have all the components
that are in Google Cloud.

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:18.740
You have everything from, like
I said, Kubernetes Engine,

00:22:18.740 --> 00:22:22.850
it's responsible for training
coordination and augmentation,

00:22:22.850 --> 00:22:25.100
Google Cloud Storage,
that's responsible for all

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:28.240
the persistence,
ML Engine that's

00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:30.550
doing the actual
training itself,

00:22:30.550 --> 00:22:32.930
and then finally, what
you saw as Aaron initiated

00:22:32.930 --> 00:22:36.500
the entire process
using Google Home,

00:22:36.500 --> 00:22:39.170
initiated through Dialogflow.

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:42.940
And that's also
represented here.

00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:46.080
So let's talk about the
main underlying concepts

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:49.920
that's required as
part of this, as far

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:51.780
as what you've seen today.

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:56.070
And that is object localization,
or object detection,

00:22:56.070 --> 00:22:58.110
commonly referred to.

00:22:58.110 --> 00:22:59.970
There are a lot of
open source libraries

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:02.500
that allow you to
do object detection.

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:04.830
But the one that was
selected for this project

00:23:04.830 --> 00:23:07.570
was TensorFlow
Object Detection API.

00:23:07.570 --> 00:23:10.440
This is an open source API
that's provided by Google

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:12.240
and is on GitHub.

00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:14.700
And it essentially
provides bounding boxes

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:19.420
around images as well as what's
called instant segmentation.

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:22.200
What I mean by bounding boxes
is what you see in this picture

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:25.650
are those boxes around
the images themselves

00:23:25.650 --> 00:23:27.510
that identify what
those objects are.

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:32.710
Like I said, there's a lot
of object detection models.

00:23:32.710 --> 00:23:37.950
But let's drill in just
quickly on a couple examples

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:40.130
and how this all works together.

00:23:40.130 --> 00:23:41.760
There are two steps
to this process.

00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:46.080
There's actually quite a few,
but the first big step here

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:48.840
is actually identifying
candidates for objects

00:23:48.840 --> 00:23:53.660
that you're going to want to
do some detection process on.

00:23:53.660 --> 00:23:57.140
And let's talk about
single shot detection.

00:23:57.140 --> 00:24:01.550
Single shot detection
is a very fast method

00:24:01.550 --> 00:24:03.770
of identifying
objects and images

00:24:03.770 --> 00:24:06.770
by using a small convolutional
network to come up

00:24:06.770 --> 00:24:08.960
with candidates of
possible objects

00:24:08.960 --> 00:24:10.910
that could be in that picture.

00:24:10.910 --> 00:24:13.310
This is really useful
for small form factor,

00:24:13.310 --> 00:24:19.300
low computational power systems
such as cell phones or TPUs,

00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:22.230
edge TPUs.

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:25.680
It's not so good at
detecting small objects,

00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:29.460
but it's fine at detecting
larger objects and pictures.

00:24:29.460 --> 00:24:31.620
The second method
is Faster R-CNN.

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:34.740
And this works differently
than SSD in that

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:37.020
it doesn't use a single shot.

00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:39.660
It actually does
post-processing of the image

00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:43.200
to find potential candidates
in those pictures.

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:46.680
And the way it works is finding
anchor points in those images

00:24:46.680 --> 00:24:48.780
and detecting foreground
versus background

00:24:48.780 --> 00:24:52.890
to come up with those
possible candidates of objects

00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:54.450
in those images.

00:24:54.450 --> 00:24:56.550
Once those candidates
are identified,

00:24:56.550 --> 00:24:58.980
the second step is actually
to do the prediction.

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:02.920
That is, what is in
those objects themselves.

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:04.830
And that's the
same whether you're

00:25:04.830 --> 00:25:08.400
using SSD or Faster R-CNN.

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:10.710
Let's talk about two possible
methods for doing that.

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:13.220
There's quite a few,
but these are two that

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:14.790
are probably the most common.

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:16.400
The first is ResNet.

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:20.120
ResNet is a deep neural network
that can go to 101 layers

00:25:20.120 --> 00:25:22.490
and beyond in
terms of its depth.

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:24.900
It's very good at
detecting detail.

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:29.580
It's very good at accuracy
of detection in an image.

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:31.370
The second is Inception.

00:25:31.370 --> 00:25:34.880
And Inception, instead of being
deep, is a wide neural network.

00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:37.160
There are also deep and
wide neural networks

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:39.350
that are Inception-based,
but essentially, you

00:25:39.350 --> 00:25:41.780
can kind of think of them
as rather than going deep,

00:25:41.780 --> 00:25:44.310
going wide.

00:25:44.310 --> 00:25:46.350
Regardless of what
neural network

00:25:46.350 --> 00:25:50.570
you decide to attach to
the detection process,

00:25:50.570 --> 00:25:52.320
you're going to want
to measure to see how

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:55.190
accurate these networks are.

00:25:55.190 --> 00:25:57.260
And the primary
way of doing this

00:25:57.260 --> 00:26:00.470
is, we're using what's called
the mean average precision.

00:26:00.470 --> 00:26:03.110
Mean average precision
is a combination

00:26:03.110 --> 00:26:06.570
of the three elements that
you see here in the slides.

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:08.210
The first being precision.

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:12.280
That is, are the
objects in the picture--

00:26:12.280 --> 00:26:14.880
are they what they're
telling me they are?

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:17.880
The second is recall.

00:26:17.880 --> 00:26:21.210
Is it seeing everything it's
supposed to see in the picture?

00:26:21.210 --> 00:26:24.330
And then finally is
intersection of union.

00:26:24.330 --> 00:26:28.940
Intersection of union measures
how much of the bounding boxes

00:26:28.940 --> 00:26:29.900
around the object.

00:26:29.900 --> 00:26:33.140
Is it too much area,
is a too little area,

00:26:33.140 --> 00:26:34.790
or is it just right?

00:26:34.790 --> 00:26:37.190
All three of those components
are combined together

00:26:37.190 --> 00:26:39.470
to come up with this
mean average precision.

00:26:39.470 --> 00:26:41.990
And you'll see this quite
often, as an example,

00:26:41.990 --> 00:26:47.480
what you see here, in terms
of measuring accuracy.

00:26:47.480 --> 00:26:49.190
The second key component
is, once you've

00:26:49.190 --> 00:26:51.410
identified those
objects, now you

00:26:51.410 --> 00:26:53.690
have to put them in real space.

00:26:53.690 --> 00:26:58.110
And how this is done
is by translating

00:26:58.110 --> 00:27:05.160
those pixels, where the bounding
boxes are, into millimeters.

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:07.500
In addition to that,
you need to orient

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:10.650
the robot, in the case of
the video that Aaron showed,

00:27:10.650 --> 00:27:14.130
to the camera so that they're
oriented in the same location

00:27:14.130 --> 00:27:16.290
at the same angle.

00:27:16.290 --> 00:27:20.240
And this is all done through
a calibration process.

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:21.950
Now, the way that
Flexible Vision does it

00:27:21.950 --> 00:27:27.140
is by using a checkerboard
to do the calibration.

00:27:27.140 --> 00:27:29.600
What I mean by calibration
is, identifying

00:27:29.600 --> 00:27:32.360
the angle of the
camera to the surface

00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:35.340
that you're doing
the detection on.

00:27:35.340 --> 00:27:37.760
In addition to
that, you want to be

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:40.070
able to align the
rotation of whatever

00:27:40.070 --> 00:27:43.715
is the device that's doing the
pick and place with the camera.

00:27:46.610 --> 00:27:49.140
And that requires a second step.

00:27:49.140 --> 00:27:51.560
In the case of Flexible
Vision, that alignment

00:27:51.560 --> 00:27:55.240
is done using a QR code.

00:27:55.240 --> 00:27:56.720
Why is this important?

00:27:56.720 --> 00:27:59.110
Because, like I said,
object detection

00:27:59.110 --> 00:28:03.430
is the basis upon
which its inspection

00:28:03.430 --> 00:28:07.780
industrial applications using
machine learning will be built.

00:28:07.780 --> 00:28:10.510
Once you've identified
an object in space,

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:12.910
you can then do OCR scanning.

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:15.310
You can then do
barcode scanning.

00:28:15.310 --> 00:28:18.400
You can then do pick and
place and quality assurance

00:28:18.400 --> 00:28:21.615
on those objects, because
you've correctly identified them

00:28:21.615 --> 00:28:22.990
and you have
confidence that they

00:28:22.990 --> 00:28:25.370
are what they say they are.

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:29.140
So that's why that
process is so important.

00:28:29.140 --> 00:28:31.870
So now we're going to go through
step by step in a little bit

00:28:31.870 --> 00:28:34.660
more detail about what's
happening under the covers,

00:28:34.660 --> 00:28:37.540
and why all these steps are
required in order to do object

00:28:37.540 --> 00:28:38.690
detection.

00:28:38.690 --> 00:28:40.810
And the reason this
is important is,

00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:43.330
it doesn't matter if you're
using the open source Flexible

00:28:43.330 --> 00:28:46.060
Vision or if you guys are
building your own tool.

00:28:46.060 --> 00:28:48.670
The steps are essentially
going to be the same.

00:28:48.670 --> 00:28:53.260
And the first step in that
process is doing the tagging.

00:28:53.260 --> 00:28:55.390
This is the idea of
dragging and dropping

00:28:55.390 --> 00:28:57.790
bounding boxes
around the objects

00:28:57.790 --> 00:29:00.880
that you're most interested
in, into pictures

00:29:00.880 --> 00:29:02.823
that you've collected.

00:29:02.823 --> 00:29:04.740
In the case of Flexible
Vision, those pictures

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:09.450
are collected from the work
cell that you're actually

00:29:09.450 --> 00:29:12.660
going to do the detection
at, or you can upload them

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:15.580
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:29:15.580 --> 00:29:18.340
In either case, once you've
collected those pictures,

00:29:18.340 --> 00:29:22.360
you're going to want to do
this bounding box process.

00:29:22.360 --> 00:29:24.880
And the bounding box
process basically

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:26.650
does three essential things.

00:29:26.650 --> 00:29:29.420
It identifies the
objects for training.

00:29:29.420 --> 00:29:31.960
It also identifies a
label that you want

00:29:31.960 --> 00:29:34.060
associated with those objects.

00:29:34.060 --> 00:29:36.460
And then finally, you're
going to select an action

00:29:36.460 --> 00:29:39.580
that you would want performed
once those objects are

00:29:39.580 --> 00:29:41.870
identified.

00:29:41.870 --> 00:29:44.300
Those actions might be
just strictly identifying

00:29:44.300 --> 00:29:46.040
the object, or it
might be, again,

00:29:46.040 --> 00:29:48.450
doing OCR scanning
or barcode scanning.

00:29:51.280 --> 00:29:52.990
One question that
comes up quite often

00:29:52.990 --> 00:29:56.020
is, how many objects
do I need, or how many

00:29:56.020 --> 00:29:59.700
pictures do I need in order
to get a good training.

00:29:59.700 --> 00:30:02.280
And that really depends
on the type of objects

00:30:02.280 --> 00:30:04.910
that you're doing detection on.

00:30:04.910 --> 00:30:08.480
As an example, luckily,
in the industrial space,

00:30:08.480 --> 00:30:11.960
many objects are well-defined in
terms of their edge and shape,

00:30:11.960 --> 00:30:14.030
and they don't vary very much.

00:30:14.030 --> 00:30:19.490
In those cases, you may
only need 3 to 10 images.

00:30:19.490 --> 00:30:23.030
In other situations, where
you're doing quality assurance

00:30:23.030 --> 00:30:26.030
and the type of failures
may vary quite greatly

00:30:26.030 --> 00:30:27.950
between different
scenarios, you may

00:30:27.950 --> 00:30:29.900
need quite a few
different images

00:30:29.900 --> 00:30:33.930
or samples of those in order
to get a good training.

00:30:33.930 --> 00:30:35.345
It may be up to 100 images.

00:30:39.190 --> 00:30:43.960
The second step to this
process requires two phases,

00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:45.750
and we call it the
training process,

00:30:45.750 --> 00:30:47.590
but it's actually two phases.

00:30:47.590 --> 00:30:51.600
The first is doing
image augmentation,

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:54.420
and the second is to actually
doing the machine learning

00:30:54.420 --> 00:30:56.190
training itself.

00:30:56.190 --> 00:31:00.720
Image augmentation is not
always required, but is really

00:31:00.720 --> 00:31:02.490
nice to have,
because what it does

00:31:02.490 --> 00:31:05.400
is it reduces the
variability associated

00:31:05.400 --> 00:31:07.380
with environmental variables.

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:10.680
Like Aaron mentioned,
if you have a skylight

00:31:10.680 --> 00:31:13.740
and the lighting might change
day to day during the day,

00:31:13.740 --> 00:31:17.140
or if the camera angle,
at the time that you're

00:31:17.140 --> 00:31:19.140
doing the training, is
different than the camera

00:31:19.140 --> 00:31:21.960
angle at the time that you're
actually doing the detection

00:31:21.960 --> 00:31:25.500
and production, having
done this augmentation

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:27.990
is a really nice thing to have.

00:31:27.990 --> 00:31:32.190
What it essentially performs
is skewing or scaling,

00:31:32.190 --> 00:31:37.620
image contrast, or rotation.

00:31:37.620 --> 00:31:42.110
Another nice aspect of doing
this augmentation process is,

00:31:42.110 --> 00:31:46.160
you might take a sample of
size of, say, 100 images,

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:49.460
and it might explode
to 1,000 images based

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:53.280
on each augmentation has a
new image in and of itself.

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:57.190
So you actually end up
with a larger sample size.

00:31:57.190 --> 00:31:59.640
And once you have
that process complete,

00:31:59.640 --> 00:32:02.350
then you would actually go
through the training itself.

00:32:02.350 --> 00:32:03.960
Now, the training
itself is fairly

00:32:03.960 --> 00:32:05.910
straightforward from
the perspective of,

00:32:05.910 --> 00:32:07.700
you've already done
all the hard work.

00:32:07.700 --> 00:32:11.100
The training is done all
by ML Engine in this case.

00:32:11.100 --> 00:32:13.020
Now, what happens
on the back end

00:32:13.020 --> 00:32:16.170
is that ML Engine
is spinning up,

00:32:16.170 --> 00:32:20.880
in the case of Flexible Vision,
32 simultaneous GPU servers

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:23.710
to perform a training
in about an hour.

00:32:23.710 --> 00:32:27.060
Now, if you compare that to what
if you ran it on your own GPU,

00:32:27.060 --> 00:32:30.720
that might take 16 hours.

00:32:30.720 --> 00:32:36.330
The other benefit of this is
that, once you do the training,

00:32:36.330 --> 00:32:40.980
if you need to retrain,
it's very quick as well.

00:32:40.980 --> 00:32:43.300
So it's a good benefit
to doing it that way.

00:32:48.280 --> 00:32:51.060
Now let's talk about
the third step.

00:32:51.060 --> 00:32:53.640
The third step in this
process is actually

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:57.910
to do the prediction
in production.

00:32:57.910 --> 00:33:02.140
In order to do this
prediction in production,

00:33:02.140 --> 00:33:06.200
let's talk a little bit
about the flow itself.

00:33:06.200 --> 00:33:07.850
And let's talk about
what Aaron showed

00:33:07.850 --> 00:33:09.680
in that first video,
that is, the picking

00:33:09.680 --> 00:33:13.520
and placing of the nut.

00:33:13.520 --> 00:33:17.460
So he initiated that
process using Google Home.

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:20.400
Google Home, then,
triggered Dialogflow,

00:33:20.400 --> 00:33:25.940
which is in Google Cloud, which
then triggered a Pub/Sub event.

00:33:25.940 --> 00:33:29.670
A Pub/Sub event is a
topic that is listened to

00:33:29.670 --> 00:33:34.410
by external sources
that contains some data.

00:33:34.410 --> 00:33:39.060
That Pub/Sub event was
listened to by Node-RED.

00:33:39.060 --> 00:33:42.330
Node-RED, again, is an
IoT workflow engine.

00:33:42.330 --> 00:33:43.800
It's open source.

00:33:43.800 --> 00:33:48.880
But you can use any IoT
workflow engine in this case.

00:33:48.880 --> 00:33:52.450
Once it was triggered, it
then called the REST API

00:33:52.450 --> 00:33:56.410
that was on the Flexible
Vision server that

00:33:56.410 --> 00:34:00.310
said, take a snapshot and
return to me the bounding boxes

00:34:00.310 --> 00:34:04.510
associated with image objects
that you found in an image.

00:34:04.510 --> 00:34:08.440
With that, Flexible Vision
then triggered a snapshot

00:34:08.440 --> 00:34:12.400
from the camera, returned
back the results that

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:14.920
were found within TensorFlow.

00:34:14.920 --> 00:34:17.500
Those results included
the bounding boxes

00:34:17.500 --> 00:34:21.219
and some binary digit associated
with the bounding boxes

00:34:21.219 --> 00:34:21.880
themselves.

00:34:24.489 --> 00:34:27.280
Flexible vision then
used that information

00:34:27.280 --> 00:34:31.760
to return back the location and
space, that is, the location

00:34:31.760 --> 00:34:36.550
in millimeters, the textual
label associated with those

00:34:36.550 --> 00:34:41.710
bounding boxes, in addition,
it will return back OCR scan

00:34:41.710 --> 00:34:45.510
or barcode scan data as well.

00:34:45.510 --> 00:34:47.540
All that data is
returned back to Node-RED

00:34:47.540 --> 00:34:50.165
in the form of a JSON document.

00:34:50.165 --> 00:34:53.929
And then finally, Node-RED
then commands the robot

00:34:53.929 --> 00:34:57.860
to move to a specific
spot based on a midpoint

00:34:57.860 --> 00:35:01.760
of a bounding box, and
then returned back the nut

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:02.360
in question.

00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:08.430
So now I'm going to turn
it back over to Aaron,

00:35:08.430 --> 00:35:09.970
and he's going to
close things up.

